TABLE products ADD COLUMN weight_grams integer;
ALTER TABLE products ALTER COLUMN currency SET DEFAULT 'EUR';
ALTER TABLE products RENAME COLUMN name TO title;
ALTER TABLE products DROP COLUMN weight_grams;
ALTER TABLE products ALTER COLUMN price TYPE numeric(14,4); -- rewrite
Trick (zero-downtime NOT NULL): adding NOT NULL directly scans the whole table under ACCESS EXCLUSIVE. Instead add an equivalent CHECK (c IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID, VALIDATE CONSTRAINT it (weaker lock, concurrent reads and writes continue), then SET NOT NULL — PostgreSQL 12+ recognises the validated check and skips the scan. PostgreSQL 18 simplifies this further by allowing ALTER TABLE … ADD CONSTRAINT … NOT NULL … NOT VALID directly.

DROP TABLE products;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products CASCADE; -- CASCADE also drops dependent views, FKs
TRUNCATE products; -- delete all rows, fast, resets nothing else
TRUNCATE products RESTART IDENTITY CASCADE;
TRUNCATE is DDL, not DML: it takes an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock, does not fire row triggers, and does not produce dead tuples for VACUUM to clean. It is transactional in PostgreSQL — you can roll it back, unlike in some other engines.

INSERT INTO products (sku, title, price) VALUES ('A-1', 'Widget', 9.99);
-- multi-row: one statement, one round trip, one WAL flush
INSERT INTO products (sku, title, price) VALUES
('A-2', 'Gadget', 19.99),
('A-3', 'Doohickey', 4.50);
-- from a query
INSERT INTO archive_products (id, sku, title)
SELECT id, sku, title FROM products WHERE discontinued_at IS NOT NULL;
For bulk loading, COPY is an order of magnitude faster than row-at-a-time INSERT:
COPY products (sku, title, price) FROM '/data/products.csv' CSV HEADER;
\copy products (sku,title,price) FROM 'products.csv' CSV HEADER -- client-side
PostgreSQL 17+ adds error tolerance to COPY:
COPY products FROM '/data/dirty.csv' CSV HEADER (ON_ERROR ignore, LOG_VERBOSITY verbose);

CREATE TABLE posts (id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY, tags text[]);
INSERT INTO posts (tags) VALUES (ARRAY['sql','postgres']), ('{"docs","draft"}');
SELECT * FROM posts WHERE 'sql' = ANY(tags); -- membership
SELECT * FROM posts WHERE tags @> ARRAY['sql']; -- contains; GIN-indexable
SELECT tags[1] FROM posts; -- 1-based indexing
SELECT array_length(tags, 1) FROM posts;
SELECT id, unnest(tags) AS tag FROM posts; -- expand to rows
SELECT array_agg(DISTINCT tag) FROM ...; -- collapse rows to array
Arrays are genuinely useful for small, fixed-cardinality, always-read-together sets (tags, permission flags, embedding dimensions). They are a poor substitute for a join table when you need per-element metadata, referential integrity, or per-element updates. Index with GIN (CREATE INDEX ON posts USING gin (tags)) if you filter with @> or && — see Post 03.

CREATE DOMAIN email AS text
CHECK (VALUE ~ '^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+\.[^@\s]+$');
CREATE DOMAIN positive_money AS numeric(12,2) CHECK (VALUE >= 0);
CREATE TABLE users (id bigint, contact email NOT NULL, balance positive_money);
A domain is a named type plus constraints, reusable across tables. Cheaper than a composite type, and it keeps validation in one place.
Migration note: when moving a relational schema to a document store, the type mapping is where fidelity is lost — numeric has no exact BSON equivalent, and array/jsonb columns collapse into the document naturally while enums do not. The SQL → MongoDB migration post walks through that mapping in both directions.

Backed by a unique index. Multiple NULLs are permitted by default because NULLs are distinct. PostgreSQL 15+ can change that:
CREATE TABLE t (a int, b int, UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT (a, b));
For conditional uniqueness — "email unique among non-deleted users" — use a partial unique index rather than a constraint, since constraints cannot carry a predicate:
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_active_uq
ON users (email) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;

ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT c FOREIGN KEY (...) REFERENCES ...
DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED;
BEGIN;
SET CONSTRAINTS ALL DEFERRED; -- check only at COMMIT
-- circular inserts, swap two unique values, etc.
COMMIT;
Deferring lets you write two rows that only jointly satisfy a constraint — swapping the sort_order of two rows under a unique index is the standard example. NOT NULL and CHECK cannot be deferred; UNIQUE, PRIMARY KEY, EXCLUDE, and FOREIGN KEY can.

CREATE TABLE t (id serial PRIMARY KEY);
expands to:
CREATE SEQUENCE t_id_seq;
CREATE TABLE t (id integer NOT NULL DEFAULT nextval('t_id_seq') PRIMARY KEY);
ALTER SEQUENCE t_id_seq OWNED BY t.id;
Its problems: the column is only a DEFAULT, so anyone can insert an explicit id and desynchronise the sequence; the sequence is a separate object requiring separate GRANT USAGE for inserting roles; and it is a PostgreSQL-specific extension rather than standard SQL.

